# Line endings. LF everywhere, on every machine.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# Git on Windows is commonly configured with core.autocrlf=true, which stores
# LF but checks out CRLF. Prettier is configured for LF, so on such a machine
# `npm run format:check` fails on a fresh clone against files nobody has
# touched — and worse, it fails *intermittently*, because only the files git
# has rewritten are affected. A `git stash pop` is enough to do it.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for this repository, so the working copy matches
# what is committed and what Prettier expects. It is a repository property
# rather than a per-developer setting, which is the point: it cannot be
# forgotten on the next clone.
#
# Every tracked file is already stored as LF, so this changes nothing about
# the history. It only stops the working copy from drifting away from it.
#
# `text=auto` leaves binary detection to git. There are no binary files in
# this repository today; when one is added it will be left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
